#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""[" -f "${BASH_SOURCE:-$0}" ] || { echo "a Python script: run it with python3" >&2; exit 126; }; exec python3 "${BASH_SOURCE:-$0}" "$@" || exit # sh/bash: hand off to python3 before reading the docstring
Is the ui-design skill configured for this install, and which version is it?

Prints ONE line and exits:

    0  configured        OK: configured (version N) ...      -> proceed
    1  NO VERDICT        the design map, or definition-of-done's config.json
                         beside it, could not be read         -> not a pass; fix its form
    2  not installed     NOT INSTALLED: <every fault, named>  -> follow INSTRUCTION.md

WHAT IS INSTALLED. The skill is prose; its one install artifact is the design
map: the `## Design map` table in this skill's CONTEXT.md in the install
context (fleetlib.paths.skill_context('ui-design')), committed in the install
repository. The check reads that file, the registry document it names, and,
where the install has one, definition-of-done's config.json.

THE ARTIFACT FORM (claude-skill section 3), so there is NO .install-state.json.
The map is one committed file, the same on every host and for every agent, so a
per-host record would restate it and could lie where the file cannot. The check
PRINTS SKILL.md's version rather than comparing a recorded one, and staleness
shows as a row: a version that asks for a new value adds its label to ROWS, and
a map written for the older version then reports that row missing.

THE PARSE IS BY CONTENT, AND THE COUPLING IS DECLARED WHERE THE VALUES LIVE.
The table is prose a person edits. INSTRUCTION.md step 2 writes a line above it
saying this check parses it by its row labels, so whoever relabels a row is told
what breaks. A map this check cannot read is NO VERDICT (exit 1), never OK and
never NOT INSTALLED: a reformat costs the measurement loudly, and it does not
pose as "every value is missing", which would send the person to re-ask values
they already gave.

WHAT A READER SEES IS WHAT IS READ. Text inside fenced code, indented code or an
HTML comment is hidden before anything is looked for, because an agent reading
the file treats it as an example or a note, not as the map: an old map left in a
comment must not be the one measured, and a table indented into a code block
must not count as written.

LEGS, each with its negative control (a case in check.test.py that must go
red) and what deleting the leg would let through:

  context   The install context resolves through fleetlib. A fleetlib refusal
            (no manifest for FLEET_CONTEXT_ROOT, no install root) is NO VERDICT:
            nothing was looked at. Only fleetlib.FleetError is caught, so a
            renamed fleetlib function is a traceback, which
            bin/installation-checks.test.py reds, never a quiet verdict.
            Control: FLEET_CONTEXT_ROOT at a directory with no manifest.
  file      CONTEXT.md is OPENED and read, never stat'ed: exists() is green on
            a file this process cannot read. Absent or unreadable is NOT
            INSTALLED naming the path and the errno. Control: the file removed.
  table     Exactly one visible `## Design map` heading, and under it, before
            the next heading, a visible pipe table of exactly two columns
            (label, value): a header row, its separator row, and rows of two
            cells. A row of more cells (an unescaped `|` in a value) is NO
            VERDICT naming the row, because which cell is the value cannot be
            told. Controls: no heading; the map only inside a fence, a comment
            or indented code; the table only inside a comment or indented code;
            prose where the table was; no separator row; two headings; a
            three-column header; a row with three cells: each NO VERDICT.
  rows      Every required label of ROWS exactly once in the file's tables, its
            value non-empty and not a `<placeholder>`. A label in two rows (one
            table, a second table under the heading, or any table elsewhere in
            the file) is NOT INSTALLED naming it and its lines: never
            first-wins or last-wins, since the copy an agent reads is not the
            copy a check would have picked. A placeholder is a `<...>` with no
            `@` and no `scheme://` in it, the form INSTRUCTION.md's template
            writes; `<ada@example.com>` and the autolink `<https://...>` are
            values. Controls: each required row removed in turn, one value
            emptied, one left a placeholder (also in the optional row), one row
            repeated each of the three ways: each NOT INSTALLED naming that
            label and no other. The optional row may be absent; a placeholder
            in it is still NOT INSTALLED, since an unedited template row is an
            unanswered question.
  registry  The registry row is one of three forms: `doc <path>` (a document in
            the install repository), `design <id>` (a page or frame in the
            design file, one token) or `link <url>` (another system, with a
            scheme). Controls: another form word, a form with no argument, a
            link with no scheme, a design id with a space: each NOT INSTALLED.
  doc       For `doc`, the path is resolved against the install root by
            fleetlib.paths.resolve_repo_path, must stay inside that root, and
            the file is OPENED and read until a byte that is not whitespace:
            a registry of blank lines registers nothing. Controls: a missing
            file, an empty file, a file of only whitespace, a directory, a path
            that climbs out of the install: each NOT INSTALLED naming it.
            `design` and `link` are checked for FORM ONLY, because nothing on a
            host opens a design-file node or another system without that tool's
            own access; the OK line says the registry was recorded, not opened.
  key       The Design file row has ONE form, whether or not the install has
            definition-of-done: the file's KEY as its first word (one enclosing
            code span allowed), then optionally its link in any form. The link
            is for people and is NEVER PARSED: a URL parse read a URL of another
            file whose path carries the key, and an unrelated host carrying it,
            as the same file, and read a Markdown link or a link with no scheme
            as a different one. A first word that is not a key (it holds a
            slash, a bracket, an angle bracket or a backtick, as a link does)
            is NOT INSTALLED. Controls: a URL, a Markdown link and an autolink
            with no key first, with and without definition-of-done's config.
  pairs     Where definition-of-done's config.json exists in the install
            context, five of its values are the same facts as five rows here
            (PAIRS), and INSTRUCTION.md says they are copied from it and changed
            together. Each pair is compared and a difference is NOT INSTALLED
            naming the pair. The key and the two page ids compare with
            whitespace stripped on BOTH sides, since a space around an id
            names no other node; stripping one side only refused a correct
            pair and accepted a padded one. The two marks compare EXACTLY, a
            trailing space included, because a mark is a literal prefix of a
            frame name and the space is part of it; the map writes a mark
            inside backticks, and the check reads every character between them.
            A null file_key says the project has no design file, which a
            design map contradicts: NOT INSTALLED. A config that is not a JSON
            object, or lacks one of the five keys, is NO VERDICT:
            definition-of-done's own checks own that file's shape, and a pair
            not compared is not a pair that agrees. Controls: each of the five
            changed in turn, another key first with a link carrying the right
            one, a key that only starts the same, a mark without its trailing
            space or with one space too many, a null file_key (NOT INSTALLED);
            the file not JSON, a key missing (NO VERDICT); the file absent (OK,
            saying nothing was compared). Green controls: the key followed by a
            Markdown link, an autolink or a link with no scheme; spaces around
            a key or a page id on either side.
  version   SKILL.md's `version` is printed in the OK line; an unreadable one is
            NO VERDICT. Control: check.test.py compares the printed version with
            SKILL.md's.

IF A NEGATIVE CONTROL PASSES, NO VERDICT. check.test.py reports a fault case
that came back OK as NO VERDICT for the whole run (exit 3), not as a failure of
one case and never as a pass: a check that cannot go red for a reason says
nothing about that reason when it is green.

THE EXIT CODE IS LOSSY; THE PRINTED LINE IS THE VERDICT. `fleet skills` shows it
as the reason a skill is not configured.

Environment: FLEET_CONTEXT_ROOT (read by fleetlib) selects the install;
otherwise the git top level of the working directory, then the calling agent's
home project, as `fleet where` resolves them.
"""
import json
import os
import re
import sys

sys.dont_write_bytecode = True  # the fleet tree is read-only on a host; write nothing into it
# realpath, never abspath: reached through an agent's skill link, abspath is the
# link's path under the agent's config directory, and no fleet tree is above it.
HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
SKILL_DIR = os.path.dirname(HERE)
FLEET_ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(SKILL_DIR)))
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(FLEET_ROOT, "lib"))
import fleetlib  # noqa: E402
from fleetlib import paths  # noqa: E402

SKILL = "ui-design"
DOD = "definition-of-done"
MAP_HEADING = re.compile(r" {0,3}## Design map[ \t]*")
# The design map's row labels, in the order INSTRUCTION.md's template writes
# them, and whether each is required. check.test.py holds the template to this
# list, so the two cannot drift; a row added here is a value every install must
# then give, which is a version bump (claude-skill section 3).
ROWS = (
    ("Design file", True),
    ("Drafts page", True),
    ("Approved page", True),
    ("Implemented page", True),
    ("Scratch area", True),
    ("In-build mark", True),
    ("Superseded mark", True),
    ("Screen-number registry", True),
    ("Design approver", True),
    ("Design-file owner", True),
    ("Code-change authorizer", True),
    ("Design-tool skills", False),
)
# The rows that are the same facts as definition-of-done's config.json: the key
# under its `figma` block, and how the two compare ("key": the row's first word,
# both sides stripped; "id": both sides stripped; "mark": exactly).
# check.test.py holds INSTRUCTION.md's table to it.
PAIRS = (
    ("Design file", ("file_key",), "key"),
    ("Approved page", ("pages", "incoming"), "id"),
    ("Implemented page", ("pages", "current"), "id"),
    ("In-build mark", ("in_build_marker_prefix",), "mark"),
    ("Superseded mark", ("deletion_marker_prefix",), "mark"),
)
DESIGN_FILE = "Design file"
KEY_WORD = re.compile(r"[^\s\[\]()<>/`]+")
LEADING_SPAN = re.compile(r"(`+)(.+?)\1(?=\s|$)")
REGISTRY = "Screen-number registry"
TOOL_SKILLS = "Design-tool skills"
FORMS = {
    "doc": re.compile(r"\S(?:.*\S)?"),
    "design": re.compile(r"\S+"),
    "link": re.compile(r"[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+.-]*://\S+"),
}
SCHEME = r"[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+.-]*://"
PLACEHOLDER = re.compile(r"<(?!%s)[^<>@]*>" % SCHEME)
AUTOLINK = re.compile(r"<(%s[^<>\s]*)>" % SCHEME)
COMMENT = re.compile(r"<!--.*?(?:-->|\Z)", re.S)
FENCE = re.compile(r" {0,3}(`{3,}|~{3,})(.*)")
INDENTED = re.compile(r"(?: {4}|\t)")
HEADING = re.compile(r" {0,3}#{1,6}(?:[ \t]|$)")
SEPARATOR = re.compile(r"\s*\|?\s*:?-{3,}:?\s*(?:\|\s*:?-{3,}:?\s*)*\|?\s*")
CODE_SPAN = re.compile(r"(`+)(.*)\1", re.S)
CHUNK = 65536


def out(code, msg):
    print(msg)
    sys.exit(code)


def declared_version():
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(SKILL_DIR, "SKILL.md"), encoding="utf-8") as f:
            m = re.search(r"^version:\s*(\d+)\s*$", f.read(4096), re.M)
    except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
        out(1, f"NO VERDICT: this skill's SKILL.md cannot be read ({type(e).__name__}: {e})")
    if not m:
        out(1, "NO VERDICT: this skill's SKILL.md declares no version, so the install cannot be "
               "said to be of one")
    return int(m.group(1))


def visible(text):
    """[(line number from 1, line)] of what a reader sees as prose: HTML comments
    blanked (an unclosed one hides the rest of the file), fenced code and
    indented code dropped. Blank lines are kept, as paragraph breaks."""
    text = COMMENT.sub(lambda m: re.sub(r"[^\n]", " ", m.group(0)), text)
    shown, fence, code, prev = [], None, False, "blank"
    for n, line in enumerate(text.split("\n"), 1):
        m = FENCE.match(line)
        if fence is not None:
            if m and m.group(1)[0] == fence[0] and len(m.group(1)) >= len(fence) and not m.group(2).strip():
                fence = None
                prev = "other"
            continue
        if m:
            fence = m.group(1)
            continue
        if not line.strip():
            shown.append((n, ""))
            prev = "blank"
            continue
        if INDENTED.match(line) and (code or prev in ("blank", "heading")):
            code = True  # indented code cannot interrupt a paragraph; it can follow a break or a heading
            continue
        code = False
        prev = "heading" if HEADING.match(line) else "other"
        shown.append((n, line))
    return shown


def cells(line):
    """A pipe-table row's cells, `\\|` kept as a literal pipe."""
    s = line.strip()
    if s.startswith("|"):
        s = s[1:]
    if s.endswith("|") and not s.endswith("\\|"):
        s = s[:-1]
    return [c.replace("\\|", "|").strip() for c in re.split(r"(?<!\\)\|", s)]


def tables(shown):
    """[(header line no, header cells, [(line no, cells), ...])] of every pipe
    table in `shown`: a `|` row directly followed by its separator row, and the
    `|` rows directly after that."""
    found, k = [], 0
    while k < len(shown) - 1:
        (i, line), (j, nxt) = shown[k], shown[k + 1]
        if line.lstrip().startswith("|") and j == i + 1 and SEPARATOR.fullmatch(nxt):
            rows, last, k = [], j, k + 2
            while k < len(shown) and shown[k][0] == last + 1 and shown[k][1].lstrip().startswith("|"):
                rows.append((shown[k][0], cells(shown[k][1])))
                last = shown[k][0]
                k += 1
            found.append((i, cells(line), rows))
        else:
            k += 1
    return found


def design_map(text):
    """(map rows, rows elsewhere) as [(line no, label, value)], or a string saying
    why there is no map this check can read."""
    shown = visible(text)
    heads = [k for k, (_, line) in enumerate(shown) if MAP_HEADING.fullmatch(line)]
    if not heads:
        return "no '## Design map' heading outside code and comments"
    if len(heads) > 1:
        return f"{len(heads)} '## Design map' headings, so which map is meant cannot be told"
    start = heads[0] + 1
    end = next((k for k in range(start, len(shown)) if HEADING.match(shown[k][1])), len(shown))
    inside = tables(shown[start:end])
    if not inside:
        return "no pipe table (a header row and its separator row) under '## Design map'"
    rows = []
    for at, header, body in inside:
        if len(header) != 2:
            return f"the table header at line {at} has {len(header)} cells; the map has two (label, value)"
        for n, c in body:
            if len(c) > 2:
                return (f"row '{c[0]}' at line {n} has {len(c)} cells, so its value cannot be told; "
                        f"write a `|` inside a value as `\\|`")
            rows.append((n, c[0], c[1] if len(c) > 1 else ""))
    elsewhere = [(n, c[0], "") for _, _, body in tables(shown[:heads[0]] + shown[end:]) for n, c in body]
    return rows, elsewhere


def unwrap(value):
    """The value without one enclosing code span (`IN BUILD - ` is the mark
    itself, trailing space and all), then without an autolink's angle brackets."""
    value = value.strip()
    m = CODE_SPAN.fullmatch(value)
    if m:
        value = m.group(2)
    m = AUTOLINK.fullmatch(value.strip())
    return m.group(1) if m else value


def file_key(value):
    """The Design file row's key: its first word, one enclosing code span removed
    and stripped, or None when that word is not a key (a link written first)."""
    value = value.strip()
    m = LEADING_SPAN.match(value)
    word = m.group(2).strip() if m else (value.split(None, 1) or [""])[0]
    return word if KEY_WORD.fullmatch(word) else None


def registry_leg(value, faults):
    """The registry's note for the OK line, or None with the fault appended."""
    word, _, arg = unwrap(value).partition(" ")
    arg = unwrap(arg)
    if word not in FORMS:
        faults.append(f"{REGISTRY} '{value}' is none of the three forms: doc <repository path>, "
                      f"design <id>, link <url>")
        return None
    if not FORMS[word].fullmatch(arg):
        want = {"doc": "a repository-relative path", "design": "one id with no space",
                "link": "a URL with its scheme (https://...)"}[word]
        faults.append(f"{REGISTRY} '{value}': '{word}' takes {want}")
        return None
    if word != "doc":
        return f"registry recorded as {word} {arg}, not opened (only that tool or system can open it)"
    try:
        root = os.path.realpath(paths.install_root())
        path = os.path.realpath(paths.resolve_repo_path(arg))
    except fleetlib.FleetError as e:
        faults.append(f"{REGISTRY} 'doc {arg}' does not resolve ({e})")
        return None
    if os.path.commonpath([root, path]) != root:
        faults.append(f"{REGISTRY} 'doc {arg}' resolves to {path}, outside the install repository "
                      f"{root}; write the path relative to the install root")
        return None
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as f:
            while True:
                chunk = f.read(CHUNK)
                if not chunk or chunk.strip():
                    break
    except OSError as e:
        faults.append(f"{REGISTRY} 'doc {arg}': {path} cannot be read ({e.strerror or type(e).__name__})")
        return None
    if not chunk:
        faults.append(f"{REGISTRY} 'doc {arg}': {path} holds no text")
        return None
    return f"registry doc {arg} opened and read"


def dod_figma():
    """(definition-of-done's figma block, None), (None, None) when its config.json
    is absent, or (None, why it cannot be compared)."""
    try:
        path = os.path.join(str(paths.skill_context(DOD)), "config.json")
    except fleetlib.FleetError as e:
        return None, f"its install context does not resolve ({e})"
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            cfg = json.load(f)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None, None
    except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
        return None, f"{path} cannot be read as JSON ({type(e).__name__}: {e})"
    figma = cfg.get("figma") if isinstance(cfg, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(figma, dict):
        return None, f"{path} has no figma block"
    for _, keys, _ in PAIRS:
        d = figma
        for k in keys:
            if not isinstance(d, dict) or k not in d:
                return None, f"{path} has no figma.{'.'.join(keys)}"
            d = d[k]
    return figma, None


def pairs_leg(values, faults):
    """("ok", the OK line's note) with each differing pair appended to faults, or
    ("nv", why) when definition-of-done's config.json exists and cannot be compared."""
    figma, why = dod_figma()
    if why:
        return ("nv", f"definition-of-done's config.json could not be compared: {why}")
    if figma is None:
        return ("ok", "no definition-of-done config.json, nothing compared")
    for label, keys, how in PAIRS:
        if label not in values:
            continue  # its own fault is already listed
        theirs = figma
        for k in keys:
            theirs = theirs[k]
        dotted = "figma." + ".".join(keys)
        if how == "key" and theirs is None:
            faults.append(f"definition-of-done's figma.file_key is null (the project has no design "
                          f"file), but the design map names one")
            continue
        if how == "mark":
            mine = unwrap(values[label])
            same = theirs is not None and mine == str(theirs)
        else:
            mine = file_key(values[label]) if how == "key" else unwrap(values[label]).strip()
            same = theirs is not None and bool(mine) and mine == str(theirs).strip()
        if not same:
            faults.append(f"{label} differs from definition-of-done's {dotted} ({mine!r} here, "
                          f"{theirs!r} there; change both together)")
    return ("ok", f"agrees with definition-of-done's config.json on {len(PAIRS)} values")


def main():
    if sys.argv[1:]:
        out(1, "NO VERDICT: usage: check (it takes no arguments); nothing was looked at")
    try:
        ctx = paths.skill_context(SKILL)
    except fleetlib.FleetError as e:
        out(1, f"NO VERDICT: no install context for this skill ({e}). Nothing was looked at, so "
               f"this says nothing about the design map.")
    path = os.path.join(str(ctx), "CONTEXT.md")
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            text = f.read()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: no {path}: the design map is not written. Follow "
               f"installation/INSTRUCTION.md.")
    except UnicodeDecodeError as e:
        out(1, f"NO VERDICT: {path} is not UTF-8 text ({e.reason}), so no design map could be read")
    except OSError as e:
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: {path} cannot be read ({e.strerror or type(e).__name__}); an agent "
               f"cannot read the design map either")

    parsed = design_map(text)
    if isinstance(parsed, str):
        out(1, f"NO VERDICT: {path} has no design map this check can read: {parsed}. The values "
               f"were not measured, so this is not a pass; installation/INSTRUCTION.md step 2 gives "
               f"the form.")
    rows, elsewhere = parsed

    faults, missing, empty, placeholder, repeated = [], [], [], [], []
    values = {}
    for label, required in ROWS:
        found = [(n, v) for n, lab, v in rows if lab == label]
        lines = sorted([n for n, _ in found] + [n for n, lab, _ in elsewhere if lab == label])
        if len(lines) > 1:
            repeated.append(f"{label} (lines {', '.join(map(str, lines))})")
            continue
        if not found:
            if required:
                missing.append(label)
            continue
        value = found[0][1]
        if PLACEHOLDER.search(value):
            placeholder.append(label)
        elif not unwrap(value).strip():
            if required:
                empty.append(label)
        else:
            values[label] = value
    for what, labels in (("missing", missing), ("empty", empty),
                         ("still a <placeholder>", placeholder), ("repeated", repeated)):
        if labels:
            faults.append(f"{what}: {', '.join(labels)}")
    if DESIGN_FILE in values and file_key(values[DESIGN_FILE]) is None:
        faults.append(f"{DESIGN_FILE} '{values.pop(DESIGN_FILE)}' must start with the design file's key "
                      f"(one word, not a link), then optionally its link")
    note = registry_leg(values[REGISTRY], faults) if REGISTRY in values else None
    state, dod_note = pairs_leg(values, faults)
    if faults:
        tail = f" ({dod_note})" if state == "nv" else ""
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: the design map in {path}: {'; '.join(faults)}{tail}. Follow "
               f"installation/INSTRUCTION.md.")
    if state == "nv":
        out(1, f"NO VERDICT: the design map in {path} is complete, but {dod_note}. Five of its "
               f"values must agree with that file, and unread they are not known to.")

    version = declared_version()
    tools = unwrap(values[TOOL_SKILLS]) if TOOL_SKILLS in values else "not recorded"
    out(0, f"OK: configured (version {version}); design map in {path}: "
           f"{sum(1 for _, req in ROWS if req)} required values; {note}; {dod_note}; "
           f"design-tool skills: {tools}")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
